@wes - I think you are missing a critical distinction: 
To most humans, “quality” is not equivalent to “value.” 
The Mona Lisa is on display in the Louvre. The value of that object is significant. Why? Because it’s an extraordinary piece of work - crafted by the celebrated artist himself.  Let’s say a highly skilled artist creates a high-quality replica - even improving on the original. Would you argue that the improved quality of this replica would result in that replica having equivalent or greater value than the original? An objective “rational” argument might be made that the replica is more valuable. But would the market pay more for it?
